    "author_association": "CONTRIBUTOR",
    "body": "### Problem\n\nI swapped to Neovim from Helix, and kept one preference - I like `i<Esc>` doing nothing, rather than moving you a character back. \n\nHowever, this mapping doesn't seem to be obeyed by multicursors. It doesn't even work in follow mode.\n\n### Steps to reproduce\n\n```\nnvim --clean\n:inoremap <Esc> <Esc>l\n:= vim.api.nvim_buf_set_lines(0, 0, -1, false, { \"echo hi\", \"echo bye\" })\n:norm WQj\ni<Esc>\n```\nObserve that the primary cursor remains where it is, while the multicursor moves to the left.\nNow, run with follow mode, repeating all initial steps, but changing the norm command to:\n```\n:norm Wqjq=\n```\nObserve that `i<Esc>` still only applies to the primary cursor.\n\n### Expected behavior\n\nIdeally, the mapping would be obeyed by all cursors, even in non-follow mode. This would be convenient for _me_. However, at the very least, it should be obeyed in follow mode. I could work around it there.\n\n### Nvim version (nvim -v)\n\n0.13.0 commit a6d91a8\n\n### Vim (not Nvim) behaves the same?\n\nno (no multicursor there)\n\n### Operating system/version\n\nNixOS unstable\n\n### Terminal name/version\n\nkitty 0.48.2\n\n### $TERM environment variable\n\nxterm-kitty\n\n### Installation\n\nnixpkgs",
